          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Propositions and their Converses"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.864,
            0.071,
            0.88,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "17. We have identified different types of quadrilaterals — squares, rectangles, parallelograms, rhombi, kites and trapezia. One can identify more types (e.g., we could create a category of quadrilaterals that have equal-length opposite sides)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.192,
            0.882,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose we have identified a category of quadrilaterals called Q, and we have to construct a quadrilateral of this type. For this, we are to use two thin sticks, put them together as diagonals so that the quadrilateral obtained by joining their endpoints is of type Q (see the Fig. 9.2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.393,
            0.289,
            0.605,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.393,
                0.289,
                0.605,
                0.405
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.451,
                0.412,
                0.526,
                0.43
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.2"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.442,
            0.699,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Suppose Q satisfies the following property."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.461,
            0.881,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If a quadrilateral is of type Q, then it has equal-length diagonals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.5,
            0.881,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) Should the two sticks be of equal length? Why or why not?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.241,
            0.538,
            0.833,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(b) Will it matter how the two sticks are put together?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.563,
            0.881,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Instead of the property mentioned above, suppose Q satisfies the following property."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.607,
            0.807,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If a quadrilateral has equal diagonals, then it is of type Q. What will be your answers to (a) and (b) now?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.371,
            0.672,
            0.628,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter Summary"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.71,
            0.715,
            0.731
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "proposition",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a statement that is true or false."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.736,
            0.881,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "converse",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the proposition ‘if P then Q’ is the proposition ‘if Q then P’."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.779,
            0.881,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It can happen that a proposition is true while its converse is false. It can also happen that both the proposition and its converse are true, or both are false."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.842,
            0.883,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Counterexamples",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are instances that demonstrate the falsity of a given proposition."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
